!! Testimony ~ Yang-Luna, wave two (verbatim)

```text
<<~ status "YANG-Luna: wave-two review complete; per-session unlock retained; browser capability claims narrowed to sourced facts; repository read-only" >>

# YANG-Luna (Lares, tasked spirit) · Persona: Ink-Clerk

## Claims checked

Platform-source access date is 2026-10-18 unless a publication date is given. “Confirmed” means the cited first-party page supports the claim as stated; “refuted” means it contradicts it; unsupported coverage remains a lead.

| Claim | From | Finding | Source |
|---|---|---|---|
| PRF is credential-associated output requested through WebAuthn, useful as a key input; support/outputs may be absent. | Both | CONFIRMED | [W3C WebAuthn Level 3 §10.1.4](https://www.w3.org/TR/webauthn-3/#prf-extension), Recommendation 2026-08-25; accessed 2026-10-18. |
| Chromium, Firefox, Safari and their passkeys uniformly support PRF; PRF support in iOS/Android PWAs. | Own/twin | LEAD | W3C specifies extension behavior, not browser/authenticator coverage. Need runtime matrix probes by browser, OS, authenticator, sync provider. |
| Apple passkeys sync across Apple devices via iCloud Keychain. | Both | CONFIRMED | [Apple Passkeys](https://developer.apple.com/passkeys/), accessed 2026-10-18; [Apple WWDC23](https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2023/10263/), published 2023. |
| A synced passkey is not device-bound custody; synced PRF output behavior is known. | Both | LEAD | Apple confirms passkeys sync, not PRF-extension output synchronization or custody suitability. I narrow my earlier assertion: classify sync as cross-device credential availability, but probe the actual PRF path. |
| SharedWorker can serve multiple same-origin contexts; its life is not durable. | Both | CONFIRMED | [MDN SharedWorker lifetime](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_Workers_API/Using_web_workers#shared_worker_lifetime) and [constructor](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/SharedWorker/SharedWorker), accessed 2026-10-18. Spec behavior permits shutdown without references; browser grace period varies. |
| ServiceWorker is an unreliable persistent vault / global session holder. | Twin | CONFIRMED (coordination only) | [Chrome extension service worker lifecycle](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/develop/concepts/service-workers/lifecycle), published 2023; accessed 2026-10-18: termination loses globals. This page is extension-specific; generic browser/PWA lifecycle coverage remains LEAD. |
| Browser storage can be evicted; persistence is not absolute by default. | Both | CONFIRMED | [WebKit storage policy](https://webkit.org/blog/14403/updates-to-storage-policy/), published 2023-08-10; accessed 2026-10-18. Also [MDN quotas/eviction](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Storage_API/Storage_quotas_and_eviction_criteria), accessed 2026-10-18: best-effort default, eviction conditions vary. |
| Apple Secure Enclave protects supported keys; Android Keystore/StrongBox can hardware-back keys, with device/API limits. | Both | CONFIRMED | [Apple Secure Enclave](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/protecting-keys-with-the-secure-enclave), accessed 2026-10-18; [Android Keystore](https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/keystore), accessed 2026-10-18. Neither proves ordinary web/PWA code gets these native facilities. |
| Native secure enclave/key store should wrap custody only where native adapter and hardware capability are measured. | Own/twin | CONFIRMED | Same Apple and Android vendor pages; Android explicitly recommends checking security level. |

## Converged scenario × vessel

In every cell a session gets its own face-bound capability in its own live boundary; sealed data may move, but only that capability opens authorized rows. “Ask” is the human route, with optional authenticator wrapper only after a local capability probe.

| Scenario | Desktop browser | Mobile browser / PWA | Container node / CLI / MCP | Herm / native / Mudlet / Unreal |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Morning | Ask in tab; PRF optional if measured. | Ask in foreground app/session; do not assume native key APIs. | Ask through owner-only route. | Herm remains floor; native surface asks its vessel. |
| Second session/reload | Ask again; SharedWorker coordinates store only. | Ask again after tab/relaunch. | New command/connection asks. | New surface session asks. |
| Two faces | Separate sessions and keys; no shared holder key cache. | Same. | Separate capabilities via owner socket. | Separate leases; no vessel-wide active face. |
| Walk-up | Explicit lock; lifecycle signals advisory. | Lock on available background signal, re-ask on resume. | Lock/terminate local session. | Platform lock can trigger lock; verify behavior. |
| Last close | Drop tab capability. | Drop session capability; eviction/reap is expected. | Drop process/session capability; floor may continue. | Drop surface lease. |
| Quiet sync | Replicate ciphertext; authorized tab opens its rows. | Same; foreground session opens after arrival. | Daemon ingests sealed bytes; session opens. | Surface transports ciphertext under a lease. |
| Unattended restart | Locked floor. | Locked floor. | Floor by default; bounded ember may raise serving tier only. | Herm floor; native key wrapper requires adapter and human recovery. |
| Spirit + watcher | Each is a distinct, narrow lease/session. | Background watcher not assumed durable. | Spirit and disk watcher each lease narrowly; no ambient root. | Attach through vessel lease; no independent custody. |

This retains my wave-one per-tab ruling and the twin’s strongest correction: browser support facts must not be inferred from a dated house scout as if each vendor page had been rechecked. Quiet sync also stays ciphertext-only until the matching session opens it. No source establishes that shared holder must be a key cache; the house’s per-session law rules that out.

## Standing disagreements and probes

- **Exact PRF availability and output continuity across synced passkeys:** twin and I both leaned toward optional PRF and treated sync cautiously, but our evidence did not establish platform details. Probe a matrix of named browser/OS/authenticator/provider combinations; register, request PRF, sync credential, then compare usable output and recovery behavior. Until then it is a LEAD, not a supported unlock path.
- **Last-session close returns node to floor:** Yin leans yes; I allow floor services to continue and previously left auto-lock as an operator fork. Measure an explicit lifecycle trace with two sessions and sealed-store reads. Keep floor service keyless; choose whether remaining non-sovereign work requires an ember policy.
- **Background lock timing:** immediate lock on a lifecycle signal is desirable, but delivery/reliability is platform-specific. Probe suspend, app-switch, process kill, and resume on supported devices; no wall-clock expiry decides authority.

## Operator-owned forks

- **Always-on ember:** lean toward floor-only restart, allowing a bounded, counted serving ember that never opens persona content. Flip only on a concrete necessity plus causal recognition, independent human route, and audit of its ceiling.
- **PRF/passkey wrapping:** optional wrapper, never sole route; no claim of device-bound custody for synced credential. Flip to supported convenience only after the probe above demonstrates output availability and threat assumptions per provider.
- **Last-session close:** lean toward returning sovereign operations to floor. Flip if a specific attended workflow needs key continuity and demonstrates two-face isolation with no persistent shared key.
- **Background/screen lock:** best-effort lock on event, not timer as authority. Flip lifecycle policy per vessel only on measured signal reliability and an explicit recovery cost decision.
- **Shared holder:** coordination and sealed operations only. No evidence or ruling supports caching session roots there; reopen only if operator changes per-tab law.
- **Native adapters / future surfaces:** use OS keystore only through a native adapter with measured hardware level; all other surfaces attach to a vessel session. Flip on tested adapter and recovery route.

